{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Dcgu8yXdYXNpBVQ67uz6Sr","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/paper_provenance.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"paper:\\\\n  title: Does Type Confusability Drive Schema-Induced Degradation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\in Training-Free\\\\n    LLM Information Extraction?\\\\n  version: 3\\\\n  generated_at: '2026-05-16T02:22:29.529376+00:00'\\\\n  source: manual mapping from registry + paper_framing.md\\\\nclaims:\\\\n- text: 'Training-free LLM information extraction---prompting models with target schemas\\\\n    without fine-tuning---degrades sharply as schema complexity grows: NER recall\\\\n    drops from 0.72 to 0.42 with 64 candidate '\\\\n  type: quantitative\\\\n  location:\\\\n    section: abstract\\\\n    context: result_statement\\\\n  id: C001\\\\n  evidence:\\\\n  - exp_id: exp001_count_baseline\\\\n    metric: recall count_1=0.720, count_64=0.418\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  deprecated: false\\\\n- text: 'We introduce the Schema Confusability Score (SCS) to quantify pairwise semantic\\\\n    overlap between types and design two complementary experimental paradigms: count\\\\n    cu'\\\\n  type: methodological\\\\n  location:\\\\n    section: abstract\\\\n    context: finding_statement\\\\n  id: C002\\\\n  evidence:\\\\n  - exp_id: exp015_scs_validation\\\\n    metric: 'SCS validated: Qwen3 rho=0.168 (p=7e-14), MiniLM rho=0.306 nonzero (p=5e-6)'\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  deprecated: false\\\\n- text: 'We presented a controlled empirical study isolating schema-induced degradation\\\\n    in training-free LLM IE into two orthogonal channels: a count channel suppressing\\\\n    recall as candidate types increase, and'\\\\n  type: quantitative\\\\n  location:\\\\n    section: conclusion\\\\n    context: result_statement\\\\n  id: C003\\\\n  evidence:\\\\n  - exp_id: exp001_count_baseline\\\\n    metric: 'Channel 1: recall 0.72->0.418'\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  - exp_id: exp007_error_taxonomy\\\\n    metric: 'Channel 2: IntraSub vs BCS rho=1.0'\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  - exp_id: exp016_fewrel_error_taxonomy\\\\n    metric: RE IntraSub vs BCS rho=0.954\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  deprecated: false\\\\n- text: We use Qwen3-32B-FP8 qwen3-2025, selected for its strong instruction-following\\\\n    capability, native structured JSON output support, and the practical advantage\\\\n    that the FP8-quantized 32B model fits on a\\\\n  type: quantitative\\\\n  location:\\\\n    section: experimental_setup\\\\n    context: result_statement\\\\n  id: C004\\\\n  evidence:\\\\n  - exp_id: exp001_count_baseline\\\\n    metric: model setup, all experiments use Qwen3-32B-FP8 primary\\\\n    tier: 2\\\\n  deprecated: false\\\\n- text: We introduce the Schema Confusability Score to quantify semantic overlap between\\\\n    types in a schema. Given a type $t$ with name string $name(t)$, an embeddin\\\\n  type: methodological\\\\n  location:\\\\n    section: experimental_setup\\\\n    context: finding_statement\\\\n  id: C005\\\\n  evidence:\\\\n  - exp_id: exp015_scs_validation\\\\n    metric: 'SCS proxy validated: both embeddings significant (all p<1e-5)'\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  deprecated: false\\\\n- text: Channel~1\\\\n  type: quantitative\\\\n  location:\\\\n    section: introduction\\\\n    context: bold\\\\n  id: C006\\\\n  evidence:\\\\n  - exp_id: exp001_count_baseline\\\\n    metric: recall 0.72->0.418 monotonic decline\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  - exp_id: exp_fewrel_formal_80rel\\\\n    metric: accuracy 0.857->0.504 monotonic decline\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  deprecated: false\\\\n- text: Channel~2\\\\n  type: quantitative\\\\n  location:\\\\n    section: introduction\\\\n    context: bold\\\\n  id: C007\\\\n  evidence:\\\\n  - exp_id: exp002_confusability_controlled\\\\n    metric: pairwise r=0.156 (p<1e-11), confusion ratio 10-28x\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  - exp_id: exp_fewrel_formal_80rel\\\\n    metric: BCS-controlled delta=-31.2pp\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  deprecated: false\\\\n- text: Training-free LLM information extraction---prompting a large language model\\\\n    with a target schema and asking it to extract structured information without any\\\\n    task-specific fine-tuning---enables rapid d\\\\n  type: quantitative\\\\n  location:\\\\n    section: introduction\\\\n    context: result_statement\\\\n  id: C008\\\\n  evidence:\\\\n  - exp_id: exp001_count_baseline\\\\n    metric: background framing, supported by all experiments\\\\n    tier: 3\\\\n  deprecated: false\\\\n- text: Second, pairwise SCS is a robust predictor of type confusion. Across Few-NERD\\\\n    (Pearson $r = 0.112$, $p < 10^{-12$) and MAVEN ($r = 0.129$, $p < 10^{-10$), embedding\\\\n    similarity between type pairs corre\\\\n  type: quantitative\\\\n  location:\\\\n    section: introduction\\\\n    context: result_statement\\\\n  id: C009\\\\n  evidence:\\\\n  - exp_id: exp002_confusability_controlled\\\\n    metric: Few-NERD pairwise Pearson r=0.112 [0.076, 0.156]\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  - exp_id: exp004_maven_bcs\\\\n    metric: MAVEN MiniLM pairwise r=0.129 [0.104, 0.156]\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  - exp_id: bootstrap_ci_analysis\\\\n    metric: CIs exclude zero for both\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  deprecated: false\\\\n- text: Third, confusability effects are robust across schema sizes, with BCS--accuracy\\\\n    slopes consistent between 16-type and 80-type pools (permutation p = 0.44)\\\\n  type: quantitative\\\\n  location:\\\\n    section: introduction\\\\n    context: result_statement\\\\n  id: C010\\\\n  evidence:\\\\n  - exp_id: exp_fewrel_formal\\\\n    metric: 16-rel BCS effect delta=-9.1pp\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  - exp_id: exp_fewrel_formal_80rel\\\\n    metric: 80-rel BCS effect delta=-31.2pp, wider BCS range produces larger absolute\\\\n      gap\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  deprecated: false\\\\n- text: Fourth, per-type confusability aggregation is confounded on homogeneous schemas.\\\\n    On MAVEN, whose 168 event types all derive from the FrameNet ontology, the correlation\\\\n    between mean BCS and per-type er\\\\n  type: quantitative\\\\n  location:\\\\n    section: introduction\\\\n    context: result_statement\\\\n  id: C011\\\\n  evidence:\\\\n  - exp_id: exp004_maven_bcs\\\\n    metric: Qwen3 per-type rho=+0.189 (p=0.021), MiniLM rho=+0.208 (p=0.011)\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  deprecated: false\\\\n- text: Fifth, confusability-aware schema decomposition fails to improve extraction.\\\\n    Our Schema-DAG-Guided Decomposition (SDGD) successfully separates confusable types\\\\n    into different batches, yet every decomp\\\\n  type: quantitative\\\\n  location:\\\\n    section: introduction\\\\n    context: result_statement\\\\n  id: C012\\\\n  evidence:\\\\n  - exp_id: exp008_sdgd_v2_fewnerd\\\\n    metric: SDGD v2 F1=0.241 vs baseline 0.445\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  - exp_id: exp008_sdgd_zero_fewnerd_r2\\\\n    metric: v1 sdgd_zero F1=0.251, also below baseline\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  deprecated: false\\\\n- text: Cross-task controlled experiments. We present BCS-controlled experiments with\\\\n    bootstrap confidence intervals on three datasets, establishing that the BCS--accuracy\\\\n    relationship is consistent across schema sizes (slope permutation p = 0.44)\\\\n  type: quantitative\\\\n  location:\\\\n    section: introduction\\\\n    context: result_statement\\\\n  id: C013\\\\n  evidence:\\\\n  - exp_id: exp_fewrel_formal_80rel\\\\n    metric: BCS--accuracy slope consistent across pools, permutation p=0.436\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  - exp_id: bootstrap_ci_analysis\\\\n    metric: FewRel count_8 delta CI [17.7, 42.7]\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  - exp_id: exp012_llama_fewrel_ch2\\\\n    metric: 'cross-model: LLaMA delta=-27.1pp (87% of Qwen3)'\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  deprecated: false\\\\n- text: We introduce the Schema Confusability Score (SCS), which quantifies pairwise\\\\n    semantic overlap between types using embedding cosine similarity, and its batch\\\\n  type: methodological\\\\n  location:\\\\n    section: introduction\\\\n    context: finding_statement\\\\n  id: C014\\\\n  evidence:\\\\n  - exp_id: exp015_scs_validation\\\\n    metric: SCS validated as proxy for empirical confusion\\\\n    tier: 2\\\\n  deprecated: false\\\\n- text: We demonstrate that pairwise SCS predicts type confusion across tasks and\\\\n    embedding models, while identifying a centrality confound that invalidates per-type\\\\n    aggrega\\\\n  type: methodological\\\\n  location:\\\\n    section: introduction\\\\n    context: finding_statement\\\\n  id: C015\\\\n  evidence:\\\\n  - exp_id: exp002_confusability_controlled\\\\n    metric: Few-NERD pairwise r=0.112\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  - exp_id: exp004_maven_bcs\\\\n    metric: MAVEN centrality confound (per-type positive)\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  deprecated: false\\\\n- text: 95\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\% CI\\\\n  type: quantitative\\\\n  location:\\\\n    section: results\\\\n    context: bold\\\\n  id: C016\\\\n  evidence:\\\\n  - exp_id: bootstrap_ci_analysis\\\\n    metric: 7 CIs all exclude null\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  deprecated: false\\\\n- text: Recall declines monotonically from 0.72 at count${\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,1$ to 0.42 at count${\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,64$,\\\\n    a 42\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\% relative drop. Precision moves in the opposite direction, rising from\\\\n    0.11 to 0.52, reflecting a shift from\\\\n  type: quantitative\\\\n  location:\\\\n    section: results\\\\n    context: result_statement\\\\n  id: C017\\\\n  evidence:\\\\n  - exp_id: exp001_count_baseline\\\\n    metric: recall 0.72->0.418, precision 0.105->0.52\\\\n    tier: 1\\\\n  deprecated: false\\\\n- text: Accuracy drops monotonically from 85.7\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\% at count${\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,1$ to 50.4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\% at\\\\n    count${\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\,80$, a near-halving when the model must discriminate among all 80\\\\n 