{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BShy3ZjrMUWSpdZEM7fn3f","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/literature_map.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Literature Map for schema-dag-extraction\\\\n# Updated: 2026-05-15\\\\n# Paper: Multi-channel Degradation Model + SDGD Framework for Training-free LLM IE\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n# DIRECT COMPETITORS — schema decomposition for LLM-based IE\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n\\\\n- id: llm-pee-2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Towards Event Extraction with Massive Types: LLM-based Collaborative Annotation and Partitioning Extraction\\\\\\\"\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: arXiv\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.02628\\\\n  role: direct-competitor\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Similarity-based type recall + type-partitioning prompting for massive event types.\\\\n    Divides candidate types into partitions to reduce prompt length.\\\\n    +5.4% event detection, +6.1% argument extraction, +12.9% F1 over mainstream LLMs zero-shot.\\\\n    KEY DIFF: partitions by similarity-based recall, NOT confusability-aware.\\\\n    Our DAG decomposition is confusability-driven, not just similarity-driven.\\\\n\\\\n- id: parse-2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"PARSE: LLM Driven Schema Optimization for Reliable Entity Extraction\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Anubhav Shrimal, Aryan Jain, Soumyajit Chowdhury, Promod Yenigalla]\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: EMNLP 2025 Industry\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.08623\\\\n  role: direct-competitor\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    ARCHITECT autonomously rewrites JSON schemas for LLM consumption.\\\\n    SCOPE uses reflection-based extraction with guardrails. +64.7% on SWDE.\\\\n    Treats schema optimization as black-box LLM rewriting, not principled decomposition.\\\\n    Does NOT model type confusability explicitly.\\\\n\\\\n- id: dee-2024\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Decompose, Enrich, and Extract! Schema-aware Event Extraction using LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: arXiv\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.01045\\\\n  role: direct-competitor\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Decomposes event extraction into detection + argument extraction.\\\\n    Dynamic schema-aware RAG for augmented prompts.\\\\n    KEY DIFF: task-level decomposition (detection vs argument), not schema-level type decomposition.\\\\n\\\\n- id: crossagentie-2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"CROSSAGENTIE: Cross-Type and Cross-Task Multi-Agent LLM Collaboration for Zero-Shot IE\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Meng Lu, Yuzhang Xie, Zhenyu Bi, Shuxiang Cao, Xuan Wang]\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: ACL 2025 Findings\\\\n  url: https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.718/\\\\n  role: direct-competitor\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Multi-agent: intra-group cross-type debate (resolves entity-label conflicts)\\\\n    + inter-group cross-task debate (NER↔RE mutual refinement).\\\\n    ⚠️ MODERATE NOVELTY CONCERN: cross-type debate explicitly addresses type confusion.\\\\n    KEY DIFF: reactive debate to resolve confusion post-hoc vs our proactive decomposition to prevent it.\\\\n    Must clearly differentiate in paper.\\\\n\\\\n- id: dlisc-2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Effective and Efficient Schema-aware Information Extraction Using On-Device LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: arXiv (May 2025)\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.14992\\\\n  role: direct-competitor\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Dual-LoRA with Incremental Schema Caching.\\\\n    Identification LoRA retrieves relevant schemas, Extraction LoRA performs IE.\\\\n    Focus on efficiency for on-device LLMs, not confusability analysis.\\\\n\\\\n- id: spt-2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Schema as Parameterized Tools for Universal Information Extraction\\\\\\\"\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: arXiv (June 2025)\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.01276\\\\n  role: direct-competitor\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Treats schemas as parameterized tools for LLM tool-calling.\\\\n    Schema retrieval + generation + infilling. Novel schema-LLM interface.\\\\n    Does not address type confusability.\\\\n\\\\n- id: zeroner-2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"ZeroNER: Fueling Zero-Shot NER via Entity Type Descriptions\\\\\\\"\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: ACL 2025 Findings\\\\n  url: https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.805/\\\\n  role: direct-competitor\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Description-driven: LLM supervision → BERT student for unseen types.\\\\n    +16% F1 over LLMs on hard zero-shot. Leverages type descriptions.\\\\n    Related to how schema semantics affect extraction quality.\\\\n\\\\n- id: cmas-ner-2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"A Cooperative Multi-Agent Framework for Zero-Shot Named Entity Recognition\\\\\\\"\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: TheWebConf 2025\\\\n  url: https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3696410.3714923\\\\n  role: direct-competitor\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Multi-agent collective intelligence for zero-shot NER.\\\\n    Addresses context correlation and indiscriminate demonstration problems.\\\\n    Outperforms on 6 benchmarks including domain-specific and general-domain.\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n# METHOD COMPONENTS — techniques we build upon or relate to\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n\\\\n- id: uniner-2024\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"UniversalNER: Targeted Distillation from Large Language Models for Open NER\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Wenxuan Zhou et al.]\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: ICLR 2024\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.03279\\\\n  role: method-component\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Instruction-tuning on ChatGPT-generated NER data. 43 datasets, 9 domains, 13K+ types.\\\\n    UniNER-7B outperforms ChatGPT by 7 F1 points.\\\\n    Important baseline for universal NER and schema-at-scale challenges.\\\\n\\\\n- id: gollie-2024\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"GoLLIE: Annotation Guidelines Following Large Language Model for Information Extraction\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Oscar Sainz et al.]\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: ICLR 2024\\\\n  url: https://hitz-zentroa.github.io/GoLLIE/\\\\n  role: method-component\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Code-style schema definitions + annotation guideline following.\\\\n    Strong zero-shot on unseen IE tasks. Code-Llama based.\\\\n    Baseline for guideline-driven schema representation.\\\\n\\\\n- id: gliner-2024\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"GLiNER: Generalist Model for Named Entity Recognition using Bidirectional Transformer\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Urchade Zaratiana et al.]\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: NAACL 2024\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.08526\\\\n  role: method-component\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Compact bidirectional encoder for zero-shot NER with parallel extraction.\\\\n    Outperforms InstructUIE and larger models. Non-LLM competitive baseline.\\\\n\\\\n- id: gliner2-2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"GLiNER2: Efficient Multi-Task IE System with Schema-Driven Interface\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Urchade Zaratiana et al.]\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: EMNLP 2025 Demo\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18546\\\\n  role: method-component\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Extends GLiNER to multi-task IE (NER+RE+classification). 205M params, CPU-efficient.\\\\n    Schema-driven interface. Shows schema approach scales to multi-task.\\\\n\\\\n- id: kggen-2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"KGGen: Extracting Knowledge Graphs from Plain Text with Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Mo et al.]\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS 2025\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.09956\\\\n  role: method-component\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Two-stage decomposition: entities first, then relations.\\\\n    Entity clustering/deduplication to reduce graph sparsity.\\\\n    Schema decomposition approach for KG construction.\\\\n\\\\n- id: code4uie-2024\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Retrieval-Augmented Code Generation for Universal Information Extraction\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Yucan Guo et al.]\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS 2024 Workshop\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.02962\\\\n  role: method-component\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Python class-based schema definitions + RAG for code generation.\\\\n    Unified code-style schema for all IE tasks.\\\\n\\\\n- id: iepile-2024\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"IEPile: Unearthing Large-Scale Schema-Based Information Extraction Corpus\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Guanzheng Chen et al., ZJU NLP]\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: ACL 2024\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.14710\\\\n  role: method-component\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    0.32B token bilingual IE corpus. Hard negative schema construction =\\\\n    semantically proximate schemas to train type discrimination.\\\\n    ⚠️ RELATED: hard negatives ≈ confusable schemas, but for TRAINING, not inference-time.\\\\n    Our contribution is inference-time confusability-aware decomposition (training-free).\\\\n\\\\n- id: lost-in-middle-2024\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Contexts\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Nelson F. Liu et al.]\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: TACL 2024\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172\\\\n  role: method-component\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    LLMs degrade when relevant info is in middle of long contexts (U-shaped attention).\\\\n    Motivates schema decomposition to reduce prompt length.\\\\n    No IE-specific follow-up found — opportunity for us to be first to connect this to IE.\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n# ZERO-SHOT NER / RE BASELINES\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n\\\\n- id: self-improve-ner-2024\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Self-Improving for Zero-Shot Named Entity Recognition with Large Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: NAACL 2024\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.08921\\\\n  role: background\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Self-improving framework using LLM self-generated examples for zero-shot NER.\\\\n\\\\n- id: self-prompting-re-2024\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Unleashing the Power of LLMs in Zero-shot Relation Extraction via Self-Prompting\\\\\\\"\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: EMNLP 2024 Findings\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01154\\\\n  role: background\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Three-stage self-prompting for zero-shot RE. Generates synthetic ICL samples.\\\\n    Training-free RE baseline.\\\\n\\\\n- id: reasoning-ner-2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"A Reasoning Paradigm for Named Entity Recognition\\\\\\\"\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: arXiv (Nov 2025)\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.11978\\\\n  role: background\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    CoT-based reasoning for NER. Outperforms GPT-4 by 12.3 F1 in zero-shot.\\\\n    Shifts NER from pattern matching to explicit reasoning. Strong zero-shot baseline.\\\\n\\\\n- id: nuner-2024\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"NuNER: Entity Recognition Encoder Pre-training via LLM-Annotated Data\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Sergei Bogdanov et al., NuMind]\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: EMNLP 2024\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.15343\\\\n  role: background\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Contrastive pre-training on LLM-annotated data. Strong few-shot NER foundation model.\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n# EVALUATION / BENCHMARKING\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n\\\\n- id: familiarity-2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Familiarity: Better Evaluation of Zero-Shot NER by Quantifying Label Shifts in Synthetic Training Data\\\\\\\"\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: NAACL 2025\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.10121\\\\n  role: evaluation\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    \\\\\\\"Familiarity\\\\\\\" metric for label shift in synthetic NER data.\\\\n    Zero-shot NER scores overestimated due to train-eval label overlap.\\\\n    Quantifies semantic similarity between entity types — related to type confusability.\\\\n    KEY DIFF: measures evaluation bias, not extraction-time confusability.\\\\n\\\\n- id: guidex-2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"GuideX: Guided Synthetic Data Generation for Zero-Shot Information Extraction\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Neil Deshpande et al.]\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: ACL 2025 Findings\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.00649\\\\n  role: evaluation\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Auto-generates domain-specific schemas + guidelines + synthetic labels.\\\\n    SOTA on 7 zero-shot NER benchmarks (+7 F1). Strong baseline.\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n# SURVEYS\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n\\\\n- id: llm4ie-survey-2024\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Large Language Models for Generative Information Extraction: A Survey\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Derong Xu et al.]\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: Frontiers of Computer Science\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.17617\\\\n  role: background\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Comprehensive survey of LLM-based IE covering prompting, fine-tuning, benchmarks.\\\\n\\\\n- id: knowcoder-2024\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"KnowCoder: Coding Structured Knowledge into LLMs for Universal IE\\\\\\\"\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: ACL 2024\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.07969\\\\n  role: background\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Encodes schema knowledge via code structures into LLM.\\\\n    Related to code-based schema representation.\\\\n\\\\n- id: slot-2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"SLOT: Structuring the Output of Large Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: EMNLP 2025 Industry\\\\n  url: https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-industry.32.pdf\\\\n  role: background\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Decouples output formatting from NL task. Model-agnostic structured output.\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n# EMBEDDING ANISOTROPY / REPRESENTATION DEGENERATION\\\\n# — supports \\\\\\\"domain boundary\\\\\\\" finding: BCS proxy fails for same-domain schemas\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n\\\\n- id: gao-repr-degen-2019\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Representation Degeneration Problem in Training Natural Language Generation Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Jun Gao, Di He, Hang Li, Xin Jiang]\\\\n  year: 2019\\\\n  venue: ICLR 2019\\\\n  url: https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.12009\\\\n  role: theoretical-foundation\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    FOUNDATIONAL. Shows that likelihood maximization + weight tying causes word embeddings\\\\n    to degenerate into a narrow cone with uniformly high cosine similarity.\\\\n    Proposed cosine regularization (MLE-CosReg) to widen the cone aperture.\\\\n    LINK TO OUR WORK: This is the root mechanism behind BCS compression.\\\\n    When Qwen3-32B encodes 168 same-domain FrameNet types, the degeneration effect\\\\n    concentrates them into the same narrow cone (cosine ≥ 0.90), destroying BCS discriminability.\\\\n    Cite as the theoretical explanation for why embedding-based confusability proxies\\\\n    have a principled failure mode on semantically homogeneous schemas.\\\\n\\\\n- id: ethayarajh-contextual-2019\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"How Contextual are Contextualized Word Representations? Comparing the Geometry of BERT, ELMo, and GPT-2 Embeddings\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Kawin Ethayarajh]\\\\n  year: 2019\\\\n  venue: EMNLP 2019\\\\n  url: https://aclanthology.org/D19-1006/\\\\n  role: theoretical-foundation\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Shows contextualized representations are anisotropic in ALL layers of BERT/ELMo/GPT-2.\\\\n    Upper layers are more context-specific but still not isotropic.\\\\n    Introduces quantitative measures of anisotropy (avg cosine sim of random word pairs).\\\\n    LINK TO OUR WORK: Establishes that anisotropy is pervasive across architectures —\\\\n    our MAVEN embedding compression is not a Qwen-specific artifact but a general property.\\\\n    Supports the argument that ANY embedding-based confusability proxy inherits this limitation.\\\\n\\\\n- id: cai-isotropy-clusters-2021\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Isotropy in the Contextual Embedding Space: Clusters and Manifolds\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Xingyu Cai, Jiaji Huang, Yuchen Bian, Kenneth Church]\\\\n  year: 2021\\\\n  venue: ICLR 2021\\\\n  url: https://openreview.net/forum?id=xYGNO86OWDH\\\\n  role: theoretical-foundation\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    ⭐ MOST DIRECTLY RELEVANT. Key insight: global anisotropy is caused by isolated clusters;\\\\n    WITHIN each cluster, embeddings are locally more isotropic.\\\\n    Resolution: the high average cosine similarity reported in prior work is an artifact of\\\\n    mixing embeddings from different clusters — within-cluster geometry is better behaved.\\\\n    LINK TO OUR WORK: This is the exact mechanism for our \\\\\\\"domain boundary\\\\\\\" finding.\\\\n    Cross-domain schemas (ACE vs FewNERD) span DIFFERENT clusters → BCS captures real\\\\n    inter-cluster distances → proxy works. Same-domain schemas (MAVEN FrameNet types)\\\\n    fall within ONE cluster → within-cluster variance is low → cosine sim saturates ≥ 0.90\\\\n    → BCS loses discriminability. Our empirical finding is a direct instantiation of their\\\\n    theoretical framework applied to type-level (not token-level) embeddings.\\\\n\\\\n- id: godey-anisotropy-inherent-2024\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Anisotropy Is Inherent to Self-Attention in Transformers\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Nathan Godey, Éric de la Clergerie, Benoît Sagot]\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: EACL 2024\\\\n  url: https://aclanthology.org/2024.eacl-long.3/\\\\n  role: theoretical-foundation\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Proves anisotropy is inherent to the self-attention mechanism itself, not just a\\\\n    consequence of cross-entropy training on long-tailed token distributions.\\\\n    Observed across modalities (NLP, vision), ruling out data-distribution explanations.\\\\n    LINK TO OUR WORK: Strengthens the argument that BCS compression is a fundamental\\\\n    architectural limitation, not fixable by better prompting or model choice.\\\\n    Supports our position that structural decomposition (SDGD) is necessary precisely\\\\n    because the embedding space has inherent geometric constraints.\\\\n\\\\n- id: rajaee-cluster-isotropy-2021\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"A Cluster-based Approach for Improving Isotropy in Contextual Embedding Space\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Sara Rajaee, Mohammad Taher Pilehvar]\\\\n  year: 2021\\\\n  venue: ACL 2021\\\\n  url: https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-short.73/\\\\n  role: theoretical-foundation\\\\n  relevance_note: >\\\\n    Shows local (within-cluster) assessment of isotropy is more accurate than global assessment.\\\\n    Cluster-specific dominant directions encode structural (not semantic) information;\\\\n    removing them improves semantic task performance.\\\\n    LINK TO OUR WORK: Supports that within a semantic domain (one cluster), the dominant\\\\n    directions capture domain-level features rather than fine-grained type distinctions.\\\\n    This explains why MAVEN type embeddings are compressed: the shared \\\\\\\"event-ness\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dominates over type-specific semantics in the local embedding geometry.\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n# NOVELTY THREAT ASSESSMENT (2026-05-15)\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n#\\\\n# 1. \\\\\\\"Type count vs type confusability\\\\\\\" disentangling:\\\\n#    ✅ NOVELTY SAFE — No prior work explicitly disentangles these two factors.\\\\n#    - IEPILE hard negatives: closest, but for training, not inference-time decomposition.\\\\n#    - Familiarity metric: measures label similarity for evaluation bias, not extraction.\\\\n#    - CROSSAGENTIE: resolves type confusion via debate, doesn't model as degradation channel.\\\\n#    Our multi-channel degradation model (count + confusability + context) is novel.\\\\n#\\\\n# 2. Schema decomposition baselines to compare:\\\\n#    - LLM-PEE (similarity partitioning) — MUST compare\\\\n#    - PARSE (LLM-driven schema rewriting) — MUST compare\\\\n#    - DEE (task-level decomposition) — discuss in related work\\\\n#    - SPT (schema as tools) — discuss in related work\\\\n#    - DLISC (Dual-LoRA schema caching) — discuss in related work\\\\n#\\\\n# 3. \\\\\\\"Lost in the Middle\\\\\\\" for IE:\\\\n#    ✅ OPPORTUNITY — No follow-up work applying it to IE task found.\\\\n#    We can be first to connect positional bias to schema prompt design.\\\\n#\\\\n# 4. Multi-agent approaches (CROSSAGENTIE, CMAS):\\\\n#    Growing area. Position clearly: we reduce confusion proactively via decomposition,\\\\n#    they resolve it reactively via debate. Complementary, not conflicting.\\\\n#\\\\n# 5. Overall novelty position:\\\\n#    STRONG — no prior work proposes (a) a formal multi-channel degradation model for\\\\n#    schema complexity in LLM IE, or (b) a DAG-based schema decomposition optimized\\\\n#    for type confusability. Main risk is CROSSAGENTIE's cross-type debate framing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 18972, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PzLPfiA6Mof2BkiveF4A9J","session_id":"08b36851-eb50-4863-8dbd-f6e8edd972e2","uuid":"35529c0f-7e10-4571-adfd-acf875b894af","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:08:02.887Z"}
