{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KrYmyDYq99ask82braWqcu","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/writing_plan.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Writing Plan — schema-dag-extraction\\\\n# Generated: 2026-05-15\\\\n# Venue: EMNLP 2026 (ARR May 25 cycle)\\\\n# Format: 8 pages main text + unlimited references\\\\n# Mode: Full Draft (2-claim analysis paper + SDGD negative result)\\\\n\\\\nmeta:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Does Type Confusability Drive Schema-Induced Degradation in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction?\\\\\\\"\\\\n  venue: EMNLP 2026 Main\\\\n  submission_deadline: \\\\\\\"2026-05-25\\\\\\\"\\\\n  page_limit: 8\\\\n  template: \\\\\\\"acl2023\\\\\\\"  # ARR uses ACL rolling review LaTeX template\\\\n  template_note: \\\\\\\"No EMNLP 2026 template found locally. Using acl2023.sty (standard ARR template). Worker must download from https://github.com/acl-org/acl-style-files if not available.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  primary_model: \\\\\\\"Qwen3-32B-FP8\\\\\\\"\\\\n  secondary_model: \\\\\\\"LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct (exp-012, D022: 70B→8B cross-scale validation)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  datasets: [\\\\\\\"Few-NERD (66 types)\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"FewRel (80 relations)\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"MAVEN (168 event types)\\\\\\\"]\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n# PAPER OUTLINE\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n\\\\npaper_outline:\\\\n  - section: abstract\\\\n    word_budget: 250\\\\n    dependencies: [introduction, experiments, results, sdgd_negative, conclusion]\\\\n    key_content:\\\\n      - Problem: training-free LLM IE degrades on complex schemas; prior work conflates count and confusability\\\\n      - Method: first controlled empirical study isolating type confusability from count across 3 tasks\\\\n      - Findings: multi-channel degradation (count→recall, confusability→type confusion); pairwise SCS robust cross-task (r=0.11-0.13); effect amplifies 3.4× with schema complexity; per-type aggregation confounded on homogeneous schemas\\\\n      - Negative result: naive confusability-aware decomposition fails due to merge amplification\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Must report r² (~1.5%) and frame as 'one factor among many but first empirically isolated'\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - section: introduction\\\\n    word_budget: 1200\\\\n    dependencies: []\\\\n    key_content:\\\\n      - Hook: training-free LLM IE enables rapid deployment but degrades 20-60 F1 on complex schemas\\\\n      - Gap: all prior decomposition approaches (UniNER per-type, LLM-PEE similarity partitioning, PARSE schema rewriting) operate on unvalidated assumptions about WHAT makes schemas complex\\\\n      - RQ1: Does type confusability independently predict degradation beyond count?\\\\n      - RQ2: Through what mechanisms do count and confusability degrade performance?\\\\n      - RQ3: Can confusability-aware decomposition improve extraction? (→ negative result, itself informative)\\\\n      - Contributions list (4 items): multi-channel model, pairwise robustness + centrality confound, cross-task validation, SDGD negative result identifying merge amplification\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Address Dream feedback: frame effect size honestly from the start — 'partial but first isolated signal'\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dream_fixes:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Effect size contextualization: r=0.11-0.13 explains ~1.5% variance; present as 'one factor among many but first empirically isolated'\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Mention LLM-PEE as closest prior work in intro\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - section: related_work\\\\n    word_budget: 1000\\\\n    dependencies: [introduction]\\\\n    key_content:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§2.1 Training-Free LLM IE: LLM4IE survey, InstructUIE, UniNER, GoLLIE, GLiNER, KnowCoder, GuideX, ZeroNER, Self-Improve-NER, NuNER\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§2.2 Schema Complexity in IE: LLM-PEE (MUST position extensively — similarity partitioning, not confusability-aware), IEPile (hard negatives for training), PARSE, DEE, CrossAgentIE (reactive debate vs proactive decomposition), CMAS-NER, SPT, DLISC, KGGen\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§2.3 Representation Degeneration: Gao et al. ICLR 2019, Ethayarajh EMNLP 2019, Cai et al. ICLR 2021 (cluster isotropy — directly explains centrality confound), Godey et al. EACL 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§2.4 Context Length Effects: Lost in the Middle (TACL 2024) — first connection to IE schema prompts\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dream_fixes:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"25+ references required — literature_map has 30+ entries, use all verified\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"LLM-PEE positioning: discuss similarity partitioning vs confusability-aware decomposition, why no head-to-head (different task focus), our contribution is controlled analysis\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"CrossAgentIE: reactive debate (post-hoc resolution) vs our proactive decomposition (prevention)\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - section: experimental_setup\\\\n    word_budget: 900\\\\n    dependencies: [introduction]\\\\n    key_content:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§3.1 Tasks and Datasets: Few-NERD (NER, 66 types, cross-domain), FewRel (RE, 80 relations, cross-domain), MAVEN (ED, 168 event types, same-domain FrameNet). Dataset statistics table.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§3.2 Schema Confusability Score (SCS): formal definition of pairwise SCS, Batch Confusability Score (BCS). Embedding models: Qwen3-32B hidden states, all-MiniLM-L6-v2.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§3.3 Experimental Design: Exp1 count curve, Exp2 BCS-controlled factorial design. Formal definitions of all evaluation metrics.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§3.4 Evaluation Metrics: F1 (NER, token-level text match), Accuracy (RE, exact label match), Relaxed F1 (ED — FORMAL DEFINITION REQUIRED: trigger word overlap + correct event type). Justify cross-task metric differences.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§3.5 Model and Inference: Qwen3-32B-FP8 via vLLM, prompt template, decoding params.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dream_fixes:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"MAVEN 'relaxed F1' must have formal definition in §3.4\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Cross-task metric differences must be justified\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Sample sizes: 5 batches per count level, 1000 samples, explicitly stated\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Bootstrap CI methodology described here\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - section: results\\\\n    word_budget: 1500\\\\n    dependencies: [experimental_setup]\\\\n    key_content:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§4.1 Count-Degradation Curves (Exp1): Few-NERD recall 0.72→0.42, FewRel acc 85.7%→50.4%, MAVEN relaxed F1 stable. Fig 1 multi-task count curves.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§4.2 Pairwise Confusability → Type Confusion (Claim 1): Few-NERD r=0.112 [0.076,0.156], MAVEN MiniLM r=0.129 [0.104,0.156]. Report r² throughout. SCS validation (exp-015): Qwen3 ρ=0.168, MiniLM ρ=0.306 nonzero.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§4.3 BCS-Controlled Experiments (Claim 1 continued): FewRel 80-rel Δ=-31.2pp [17.7,42.7]. Scaling 3.4× vs 16-rel. Tab main results.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§4.4 Multi-Channel Degradation (Claim 2): Error taxonomy — IntraSub 0%→49.4% (NER, ρ=1.0), 0%→32.3% (RE, ρ=0.954). Missed rate flat. Fig error taxonomy.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§4.5 Centrality Confound on Homogeneous Schemas: MAVEN per-type positive direction. Explanation via Cai et al. 2021 cluster isotropy. Pairwise still works → aggregation is the problem, not the signal.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"All CIs from bootstrap_ci_analysis. Report r² for every correlation.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dream_fixes:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Report r² throughout — r=0.112 → r²=0.013 (~1.3% variance explained)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Discuss other contributing factors: entity frequency, prompt position, type definition quality\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Frame confusability as 'one factor among many but first empirically isolated'\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Bootstrap CI for all key metrics (already computed)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Sample size limitations: 5 batches per count acknowledged\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - section: sdgd_negative\\\\n    word_budget: 500\\\\n    dependencies: [results]\\\\n    key_content:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§5 Why Naive Decomposition Fails: SDGD-zero F1=0.241 vs single-pass 0.445. Decomposition boosts recall (0.19→0.31) but kills precision (0.32→0.21).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Merge amplification mechanism: decomposed batches produce overlapping entity predictions → union merge inflates FP.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Confidence-gated merge (v2) did not help: 0.241 vs 0.251 (v1).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Comparison with random_batch (0.320) and per_type (0.292): ALL decomposition modes worse than single-pass.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Structural insight: merge is the bottleneck, not decomposition quality.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Connection to LLM-PEE: both are similarity-based partitioning; our controlled negative result suggests the merge problem is general.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Future directions: calibration-based approaches, confidence-weighted extraction.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dream_fixes:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"SDGD threshold τ=0.8 sensitivity: acknowledge no ablation in limitations\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Position relative to LLM-PEE: same fundamental challenge (merge amplification)\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - section: discussion\\\\n    word_budget: 600\\\\n    dependencies: [results, sdgd_negative]\\\\n    key_content:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§6.1 Effect Size and Practical Implications: r²≈1.3-1.7% — confusability is one factor among many. Other factors: entity frequency, prompt position effects (Lost in the Middle), type definition quality, model capacity. First empirically isolated signal, not the only one.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§6.2 Embedding Choice: 2×3 ablation matrix summary. No single embedding dominates. Extraction-model embeddings better for cross-domain (Qwen3 on Few-NERD), generic embeddings for same-domain (MiniLM on MAVEN).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"§6.3 Limitations: (a) single primary model (Qwen3-32B), LLaMA pending; (b) 5 batches per count level → limited statistical power for factorial ANOVA; (c) SDGD τ=0.8 no ablation; (d) 3 datasets may not cover all IE scenarios; (e) text_match evaluation on NER (not exact span).\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dream_fixes:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"LLaMA non-monotonicity: if exp-012 done → Patch. If not, acknowledge 'multi-model validation pending' in limitations\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Sample size: report CI widths, acknowledge ANOVA power insufficient\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"SDGD threshold sensitivity acknowledged\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - section: conclusion\\\\n    word_budget: 250\\\\n    dependencies: [introduction, results, sdgd_negative, discussion]\\\\n    key_content:\\\\n      - Restate multi-channel degradation model\\\\n      - Pairwise confusability as robust cross-task signal\\\\n      - Centrality confound as methodological insight\\\\n      - SDGD negative result → merge amplification → future calibration-based work\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Tempered claims: 'partial explanatory power' not 'comprehensive model'\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n# VISUALIZATION PLAN\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n\\\\nvisualization_plan:\\\\n  - fig_id: fig_count_curves\\\\n    type: line_plot\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"Multi-task count-degradation curves. 3 subplots: (a) Few-NERD recall vs count, (b) FewRel accuracy vs count (80-rel), (c) MAVEN relaxed F1 vs count. Error bars from 5 batches per count. X-axis: type/relation count. Y-axis: metric. Shows Channel 1 (count→recall decline).\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: \\\\\\\"Section 4.1 (Results)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"3:1 (three horizontal subplots)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: exp001_count_baseline (Few-NERD), exp_fewrel_formal_80rel (FewRel), exp004_maven_bcs (MAVEN)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    output_path: \\\\\\\"projects/schema-dag-extraction/figures/paper/fig_count_curves.pdf\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Include bootstrap CI as shaded region where available. MAVEN shows non-monotonic pattern — plot honestly.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - fig_id: fig_error_taxonomy\\\\n    type: grouped_bar\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"Error type distribution across BCS bins. 2 subplots: (a) Few-NERD NER, (b) FewRel RE. Stacked/grouped bars showing correct%, wrong_type% (IntraSub%), missed%. BCS bins on x-axis. Shows Channel 2 (confusability→type confusion) and cross-task IntraSub replication.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: \\\\\\\"Section 4.4 (Multi-Channel Degradation)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"2:1 (two horizontal subplots)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: exp007_error_taxonomy (Few-NERD), exp016_fewrel_error_taxonomy (FewRel)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    output_path: \\\\\\\"projects/schema-dag-extraction/figures/paper/fig_error_taxonomy.pdf\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Highlight IntraSub rate monotonic increase. Include ρ values on plot.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - fig_id: fig_bcs_scaling\\\\n    type: grouped_bar\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"BCS effect scaling with schema complexity. Grouped bar: 16-rel vs 80-rel on FewRel. For each: low-BCS accuracy, high-BCS accuracy, Δ. Shows 3.4× amplification. Include CI error bars.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: \\\\\\\"Section 4.3 (BCS-Controlled)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"1:1\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: exp_fewrel_formal (16-rel), exp_fewrel_formal_80rel (80-rel), bootstrap_ci_analysis\\\\\\\"\\\\n    output_path: \\\\\\\"projects/schema-dag-extraction/figures/paper/fig_bcs_scaling.pdf\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Annotate Δ values and 3.4× amplification factor.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - fig_id: fig_sdgd_comparison\\\\n    type: bar_chart\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"SDGD vs baselines on Few-NERD. Grouped bars showing F1, Precision, Recall for 4 modes: single_pass, random_batch, sdgd_zero, per_type. Clearly shows decomposition boosts recall but kills precision.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: \\\\\\\"Section 5 (SDGD Negative Result)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"4:3\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: exp008_sdgd_zero_fewnerd_r2\\\\\\\"\\\\n    output_path: \\\\\\\"projects/schema-dag-extraction/figures/paper/fig_sdgd_comparison.pdf\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Highlight single_pass as strongest F1. Show precision-recall tradeoff visually.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n# TABLE PLAN\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n\\\\ntable_plan:\\\\n  - table_id: tab_datasets\\\\n    type: statistics\\\\n    columns: [Dataset, Task, \\\\\\\"# Types\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"# Samples\\\\\\\", Domain, \\\\\\\"SCS Range\\\\\\\", Embedding]\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: exp-004 (MAVEN stats), exp001 (Few-NERD), exp_fewrel_formal_80rel (FewRel)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: \\\\\\\"Section 3.1\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Include mean/median/max SCS for each dataset.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - table_id: tab_pairwise\\\\n    type: results\\\\n    columns: [Dataset, Embedding, \\\\\\\"n pairs\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Pearson r\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"r²\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"95% CI\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"p-value\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"ρ (nonzero)\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: exp002_confusability_controlled, exp004_maven_bcs, exp015_scs_validation, bootstrap_ci_analysis\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: \\\\\\\"Section 4.2\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"MUST include r² column per Dream feedback. All CIs from bootstrap.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - table_id: tab_bcs_controlled\\\\n    type: results\\\\n    columns: [Dataset, \\\\\\\"Schema Size\\\\\\\", Count, \\\\\\\"Low-BCS Acc\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"High-BCS Acc\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Δ (pp)\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"95% CI\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: exp_fewrel_formal, exp_fewrel_formal_80rel, bootstrap_ci_analysis\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: \\\\\\\"Section 4.3\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Show 16-rel → 80-rel scaling. Include bootstrap CI for Δ.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - table_id: tab_error_taxonomy\\\\n    type: results\\\\n    columns: [Task, BCS Bin, \\\\\\\"Correct%\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"IntraSub%\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Missed%\\\\\\\", ρ]\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: exp007_error_taxonomy, exp016_fewrel_error_taxonomy\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: \\\\\\\"Section 4.4\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Cross-task comparison. Highlight ρ=1.0 (NER) and ρ=0.954 (RE).\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - table_id: tab_sdgd\\\\n    type: results\\\\n    columns: [Mode, Batches, F1, Precision, Recall, \\\\\\\"Time (s)\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: exp008_sdgd_zero_fewnerd_r2\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: \\\\\\\"Section 5\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Include single_pass, random_batch, sdgd_zero, per_type. Bold single_pass F1.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n# LITERATURE STRATEGY\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n\\\\nliterature_strategy:\\\\n  citation_budget: 30\\\\n  macro_context: \\\\\\\"Training-free LLM IE is gaining traction for rapid deployment but quality gaps with supervised methods remain poorly understood. We provide the first controlled factor analysis.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  micro_clusters:\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"training_free_llm_ie\\\\\\\"\\\\n      description: \\\\\\\"Training-free / zero-shot LLM-based IE methods\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 8\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - uniner-2024      # ICLR 2024, per-type splitting baseline\\\\n        - gollie-2024       # ICLR 2024, code-style schema\\\\n        - gliner-2024       # NAACL 2024, non-LLM competitive baseline\\\\n        - knowcoder-2024    # ACL 2024, code-based schema knowledge\\\\n        - zeroner-2025      # ACL 2025 Findings\\\\n        - guidex-2025       # ACL 2025 Findings\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"zero-shot LLM information extraction 2024 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"training-free named entity recognition large language model\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"schema_decomposition\\\\\\\"\\\\n      description: \\\\\\\"Schema decomposition / complexity management for IE\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 8\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - llm-pee-2025      # CRITICAL: closest competitor, embedding similarity partitioning\\\\n        - iepile-2024        # ACL 2024, hard-negative schema\\\\n        - crossagentie-2025  # ACL 2025 Findings, multi-agent type confusion\\\\n        - kggen-2025         # NeurIPS 2025, two-stage decomposition\\\\n        - parse-2025         # EMNLP 2025 Industry\\\\n        - dee-2024           # task-level decomposition\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"schema decomposition information extraction LLM\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"type confusion resolution information extraction\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"representation_degeneration\\\\\\\"\\\\n      description: \\\\\\\"Embedding anisotropy and representation degeneration literature\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 5\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - gao-repr-degen-2019     # ICLR 2019, foundational\\\\n        - ethayarajh-contextual-2019  # EMNLP 2019, contextual anisotropy\\\\n        - cai-isotropy-clusters-2021  # ICLR 2021, cluster isotropy — KEY for centrality confound\\\\n        - godey-anisotropy-inherent-2024  # EACL 2024\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"embedding anisotropy transformer representation degeneration\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"context_length_ie\\\\\\\"\\\\n      description: \\\\\\\"Context length effects and positional bias\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 2\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - lost-in-middle-2024   # TACL 2024\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"lost in the middle long context language model\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"ie_surveys_benchmarks\\\\\\\"\\\\n      description: \\\\\\\"IE surveys and evaluation methodology\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 4\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - llm4ie-survey-2024    # comprehensive survey\\\\n        - familiarity-2025      # NAACL 2025, evaluation methodology\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"LLM information extraction survey 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"NER evaluation methodology zero-shot\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"instructuie_baselines\\\\\\\"\\\\n      description: \\\\\\\"Instruction-tuned universal IE baselines\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 3\\\\n      must_cite: []\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"InstructUIE instruction-tuned information extraction\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"universal information extraction instruction tuning 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n# WRITING ORDER (strict serial)\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n\\\\nwriting_order:\\\\n  - section: experimental_setup\\\\n    rationale: \\\\\\\"Establishes methodology, metric definitions, and dataset descriptions. Foundation for all subsequent sections.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    worker_type: paper_writing\\\\n    worker_inputs:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"paper_framing.md §Experimental Design\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"registry: exp001, exp002, exp004, exp_fewrel_formal_80rel (design fields)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"latex-paper skill + red-section skill\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Dream feedback: formal metric definitions, sample size disclosure\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - section: results\\\\n    rationale: \\\\\\\"Core empirical findings. Depends on experimental_setup for metric definitions.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    worker_type: paper_writing\\\\n    worker_inputs:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"experimental_setup.tex (completed)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"registry: ALL successful experiments (metrics fields)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"bootstrap_ci_analysis (all CIs)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"paper_framing.md §Evidence Hierarchy\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Dream feedback: r² throughout, effect size contextualization\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - section: sdgd_negative\\\\n    rationale: \\\\\\\"Negative result section. Depends on results for context.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    worker_type: paper_writing\\\\n    worker_inputs:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"results.tex (completed)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"registry: exp008_sdgd_zero_fewnerd_r2, exp008_sdgd_v2_fewnerd\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Dream feedback: SDGD threshold sensitivity, LLM-PEE connection\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - section: related_work\\\\n    rationale: \\\\\\\"Positioned after empirical sections to ensure accurate framing. 25+ refs required.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    worker_type: paper_writing\\\\n    worker_inputs:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"introduction.tex (not yet, but outline available)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"literature_map.yaml (full)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"references.bib (after Step 3)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Dream feedback: LLM-PEE extensive positioning, CrossAgentIE differentiation\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - section: introduction\\\\n    rationale: \\\\\\\"Written after results and related work are stable to ensure accurate claims.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    worker_type: paper_writing\\\\n    worker_inputs:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"results.tex + sdgd_negative.tex + related_work.tex (all completed)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"paper_framing.md §Story Spine\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Dream feedback: honest effect size framing from the start\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - section: conclusion\\\\n    rationale: \\\\\\\"Mirrors introduction. Written after all body sections.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    worker_type: paper_writing\\\\n    worker_inputs:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"All completed .tex sections\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Dream feedback: tempered claims\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - section: abstract\\\\n    rationale: \\\\\\\"Last to write — summarizes the complete paper.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    worker_type: paper_writing\\\\n    worker_inputs:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"All completed .tex sections\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Dream feedback: r² in abstract, honest framing\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n# DREAM REVIEW ISSUES TRACKING\\\\n# =============================================================================\\\\n\\\\ndream_review_fixes:\\\\n  - id: DR1\\\\n    issue: \\\\\\\"Related work insufficient (need 25+ refs)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    severity: critical\\\\n    addressed_in: [related_work]\\\\n    strategy: \\\\\\\"literature_map has 30+ entries. Step 3 will gap-fill and verify all.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: DR2\\\\n    issue: \\\\\\\"Effect size not contextualized (r²~1.5%)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    severity: critical\\\\n    addressed_in: [abstract, introduction, results, discussion]\\\\n    strategy: \\\\\\\"Report r² everywhere. Frame as 'one factor among many, first empirically isolated'. Discuss other factors in §6.1.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: DR3\\\\n    issue: \\\\\\\"MAVEN relaxed F1 needs formal definition\\\\\\\"\\\\n    severity: major\\\\n    addressed_in: [experimental_setup]\\\\n    strategy: \\\\\\\"§3.4 formal definition: trigger word overlap (any token overlap) + correct event type.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: DR4\\\\n    issue: \\\\\\\"LLM-PEE positioning missing\\\\\\\"\\\\n    severity: critical\\\\n    addressed_in: [related_work, sdgd_negative]\\\\n    strategy: \\\\\\\"§2.2 extensive discussion. §5 connection to merge amplification. No head-to-head (different task/eval).\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: DR5\\\\n    issue: \\\\\\\"LLaMA count curve non-monotonicity\\\\\\\"\\\\n    severity: major\\\\n    addressed_in: [discussion]\\\\n    strategy: \\\\\\\"If exp-012 done → Patch. If not → §6.3 acknowledge 'multi-model validation pending'.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: DR6\\\\n    issue: \\\\\\\"Sample size limitations (5 batches per count)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    severity: major\\\\n    addressed_in: [experimental_setup, discussion]\\\\n    strategy: \\\\\\\"Report bootstrap CI for all metrics. §6.3 acknowledge ANOVA power limitations.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: DR7\\\\n    issue: \\\\\\\"SDGD τ=0.8 no ablation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    severity: minor\\\\n    addressed_in: [discussion]\\\\n    strategy: \\\\\\\"§6.3 acknowledge. Note that all τ values face merge amplification (structural, not threshold-dependent).\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 22225, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01N6BRmGrcwiu7gm7j98SRfJ","session_id":"08b36851-eb50-4863-8dbd-f6e8edd972e2","uuid":"a5683c11-e6c6-4680-9902-22150ad716c4","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:08:24.017Z"}
